substantive in the sentence now then bones which is correct my father dislikes my dancing with young

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:29.960
girls or my father dislikes me dancing with young girls my pop don't like me dancing with no

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:42.610
girl. I don't blame him no how. But Bones, the use of the word with in the sentence makes it

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:47.730
prepositional. So perhaps I should read you the simple rule governing prepositional phrases

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:53.830
before I can expect you to understand possessive pronouns. Yeah, maybe that's what's clogging up my

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:59.210
mind, Miss Brooks. Maybe if you read me about them prepositional things, I might see the whole thing

00:16:59.210 --> 00:16:59.570
clear.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:00.880
Yeah, maybe.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.150
All right, don't. Eyes on the blackboard.

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:05.450
Rule.

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:10.980
A prepositional phrase, a subordinate clause, or a participial phrase

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:14.280
may take the place of a noun in almost any of its relations.

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:15.910
Thus, the sentence,

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:19.530
for him to use the society's money was dishonest,

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:23.440
affords us a case in which an infinitive, to use,

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:27.500
serves as a noun by being the object of the preposition for,

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:29.970
and at the same time, as a verb.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.580
by taking a subject in him and an object in money.

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:38.140
And the whole unites to form the subject of the verb was

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:42.360
and is qualified by the predicate adjective dishonest.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.370
Is that clear?

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:50.970
Well, Bones?

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:53.670
My pop won't even let me dance with my mother.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:10.140
And so we wave a reluctant farewell to the prepositional phrase, plunging boldly into the subjunctive mood.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:10.920
All right.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:12.610
Come with me, Bones.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:16.930
Rule.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.900
Say, I am John the Fisherman.

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.740
I am making a positive statement, flatly stating that I am indeed John the Fisherman.

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:29.970
The Fisherman acting in apposition with John the N-

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:38.080
which it explains. But if the words if I were precede the noun, that places the sentence in

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:45.320
the subjunctive mood, thus indicating that the supposition is not a possibility. Thus, if I say

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:51.900
if I were John the Fisherman, the verb were makes it obvious that I am not John the Fisherman. For

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:59.660
if I were John the Fisherman, I would simply state I am John the Fisherman. Now, if I say

00:18:59.660 --> 00:18:59.800
Okay.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:05.260
If I were John the Fisherman, am I or am I not John the Fisherman?

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.740
That's easy. You're not John the Fisherman.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.940
You're right, Bones. I'm not John the Fisherman.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.640
But how do you know I'm not John the Fisherman?

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:22.990
Because if you was John the Fisherman, you'd be carrying fish instead of that crazy stick.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:29.200
Grasp the fundamentals. On to the test.

